Review the draft formalizations of Knowledge Foundry's aml-cft-pilot-13 in a fresh
Codex session. Read AGENTS.md, .agents/AGENTS.md, doc/reasoning-engine.md,
schemas/reasoning-mapping-schema-v1.0.0.json,
src/knowledge_foundry/reasoning/adapter.py, scripts/validate_artifact_examples.py,
and src/knowledge_foundry/prompts/agent_prompt_claim_formalization_generation first.

Scope and authorization
- This is source-grounding and semantic review, not generation or repair.
  Complete the review end to end, saving judgments and progress regularly.
  This instruction authorizes new review artifacts under REVIEW only. Do not
  modify the frozen drafts, vocabulary, source inputs, schemas or product code.
  Do not commit, publish, regenerate claims or launch other agents/models/APIs.
- Treat the generator's text and confidence as untrusted proposals. Base judgments
  on source evidence, not on another model's explanation or successful parsing.

Inputs
RUN = artifacts/20261028-docling-aml-cft-01
FORMALIZATIONS = artifacts/20261028-docling-aml-cft-01/formalization-v1
REVIEW = artifacts/20261028-docling-aml-cft-01/formalization-v1-review
- FORMALIZATIONS/{manifest.json,input-bundle.json,candidate-claim-links.jsonl,
  vocabulary.json,coverage.jsonl,notes.jsonl,batches/batch-NNN.json}.
- Original candidates, evidence context, source documents and prior findings
  named in the frozen manifest; resolve originals through data/manifest.json.
If generation used another run directory, resolve its actual manifest and record
that exact path; do not review a mixture of revisions or assume files exist.
Record actual reviewer/model/reasoning settings and times; unknown settings stay
null. A model recommendation is not evidence of the model actually running.

1. Freeze and validate the review scope
- Verify manifest hashes, the complete candidate inventory, the input bundle's
  evidence lineage, candidate-to-claim links and vocabulary/batch dependencies.
  Use check_claim_evidence for the topic-free export and the mapping JSON schema
  for draft batches. Independently recompute claim/bundle/content hashes.
- Record missing/stale inputs or invalid artifacts as findings. Do not repair
  them or force schema/review gates to pass. Continue reviewing unaffected items
  whose source identity can still be established; affected items remain blocked.
- Inspect every mapping and every candidate disposition. Sampling alone cannot
  grant corpus or batch acceptance. Excluded, ambiguous and unsupported items
  need review too, so missed obligations are not hidden as exclusions.

2. Inspect original sources and formalizations
- For each claim, separately assess grounding against exact source passages,
  governing context, source version and qualifications. Inspect PDF text and
  rendered layout when reviewing PDF evidence; record actual page locations.
  Do not infer that an unreviewed claim is grounded because its formalization
  looks plausible. Preserve prior findings and locate evidence for any revision.
- Assess each fact/rule: logical strength, relationship direction, variables,
  class versus individual, obligation versus actual behavior, jurisdiction,
  effective dates, conditions, exceptions and positive clearance requirements.
  An apparently reasonable rule is not enough; it must preserve the source's
  meaning. Do not approve unsupported decree conditions or invented case facts.
- Check consistency of vocabulary across batches and documents. Synonyms do not
  automatically denote the same concept. Different editions or jurisdictions
  need explicit scope; report conflicts without inventing legal precedence.
- For supported rules, document a qualifying case, a failed-condition case,
  missing-evidence behavior and any exception case. Keep invented test cases
  explicitly synthetic and separate from regulatory or customer assertions.
- Schema success and a deduction certificate cannot establish that a rule is
  legally faithful. The existing engine accepts only reviewed mappings grounded
  in verified claims; do not mark draft inputs verified just to execute tests.
- Use pass/fail/pending. Pending requires a concrete inspection blocker and the
  attempted checks; ambiguity or unsupported representation can be a substantive
  finding. Never mark original/layout inspection true unless it was performed.

3. Save findings and hand off for repair or acceptance work
- Write review.json keyed by candidate ID, claim ID, batch and mapping ID, with
  independent grounding, formalization, scope and vocabulary judgments, located
  notes, inspection records and reviewer identity. Bind it to input/manifest hashes.
- Write findings.jsonl with stable finding IDs, severity (critical/major/minor),
  affected IDs, category, exact source/location, violated requirement, proposed
  repair direction, status and any dependencies on other batches/vocabulary.
  Do not silently fix the draft or overwrite older findings.
- Write report.json with total/reviewed/passed/failed/pending counts by dimension
  and document, checked exclusions, automatic-check results, unresolved blockers
  and affected-batch lists. Save a precise resume checkpoint if incomplete.
- An entry is only eligible for later acceptance when grounding and all semantic
  dimensions pass. A batch with unresolved failing or pending entries is not
  accepted. Do not invent a score threshold that averages away defects.
- Leave generated artifacts draft and immutable. This session records decisions;
  it does not promote artifacts, merge batches into an executable corpus, change
  original claim statuses or claim that a runtime import is implemented.
- Report whether repair is needed, the exact frozen files/findings to give the
  repair session, and which items require fresh review after correction. Repair
  never grants acceptance; changes to shared terms require reviewing dependents.

A separate session provides fresh context, not guaranteed model independence.
Report uncertainty and incomplete coverage candidly.
